&lt;p&gt;How I Built a Receipt Scanning PWA for French Freelancers (and What I Learned)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few months ago, I launched &lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ScanCompta&lt;/a&gt; — a Progressive Web App that lets French freelancers and small business owners scan their receipts and manage expense reports, directly from their phone or browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's what I learned building it.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Problem I Was Solving
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;France has around 4 million self-employed workers — freelancers, tradespeople, VTC drivers, consultants. Most of them handle their own bookkeeping. And they all deal with the same nightmare: &lt;strong&gt;paper receipts&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Under French law, receipts must be kept for 10 years (article L.123-22 of the Code de commerce). But since 2017, a regulation (the "arrêté du 22 mars 2017") allows a digital photo to legally replace the paper original — as long as the scan meets specific quality standards.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The problem: no one knows about this rule. And even fewer people had a simple, affordable tool that actually handled the French-specific stuff: VAT rates (there are 4 in France: 20%, 10%, 5.5%, 2.1%), URSSAF plafonds, the kilometric allowance (which changes every year), etc.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Existing tools like Expensify or Dext are either too expensive, too complex, or not designed for the French regulatory context. That was my opening.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Stack
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built ScanCompta as a &lt;strong&gt;PWA&lt;/strong&gt; for a simple reason: I wanted one codebase that works on iOS, Android, and desktop without going through the App Store review process or paying 30% fees.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's what I used:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;React&lt;/strong&gt; — component library&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;TanStack Router&lt;/strong&gt; — for SSR and file-based routing (a genuinely underrated tool, especially for SEO since it makes server-side rendering straightforward)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Lovable&lt;/strong&gt; — AI-assisted development platform that accelerated the initial build significantly&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cloudflare&lt;/strong&gt; — for CDN, security headers, cache rules, and Brotli compression&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;OCR&lt;/strong&gt; — for receipt scanning and data extraction&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The SSR part was important. A PWA that is 100% client-rendered is essentially invisible to Google. Getting SSR working properly with TanStack Router took some trial and error — their documentation for SSR patterns is still maturing — but it's worth it.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The SEO Challenge: Ranking for Hyper-Specific French Queries
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building the product was only half the battle. The harder part was getting discovered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;French freelancers don't search for "expense management software." They search for very specific things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu/blog/auto-entrepreneur-garder-tickets-caisse" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Combien de temps garder ses tickets de caisse auto-entrepreneur&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu/blog/tva-peage-essence-vtc-taxi" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TVA récupérable péage VTC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu/ticket-de-caisse-perdu" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Ticket de caisse perdu urssaf que faire&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu/blog/indemnites-kilometriques-ou-frais-reels-2026" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Indemnités kilométriques ou frais réels 2026&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I built dedicated landing pages and blog articles for each of these queries. Some examples:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu/en/blog/sole-trader-keep-receipts" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;How long to keep receipts as a sole trader&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu/en/blog/vat-fuel-toll-receipts-drivers" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;VAT on fuel and toll receipts for VTC drivers&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu/ticket-de-caisse-perdu" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;What to do with a lost receipt&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu/en/blog/mileage-allowance-vs-actual-expenses-2026" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Mileage allowance vs actual expenses in 2026&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The key insight: &lt;strong&gt;in a niche market, long-tail informational content outperforms generic product pages&lt;/strong&gt;. People landing on a "lost receipt" article are exactly the kind of users who would benefit from a receipt scanning app.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The JSON-LD Layer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One thing I spent a lot of time on that paid off: &lt;strong&gt;structured data&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every page has properly implemented schema markup:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;SoftwareApplication&lt;/code&gt; on the homepage with pricing, OS compatibility, app category&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;BlogPosting&lt;/code&gt; on every article with &lt;code&gt;datePublished&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;dateModified&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;author&lt;/code&gt; (Organization, not a person), &lt;code&gt;speakable&lt;/code&gt; sections&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;BreadcrumbList&lt;/code&gt; on every page&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;FAQPage&lt;/code&gt; on key landing pages&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;WebSite&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;SearchAction&lt;/code&gt; for sitelinks search box&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a small product competing against established players, structured data gives you a disproportionate advantage. Google's AI Overviews and Perplexity both favor sources with clean semantic markup.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Lessons for Other Indie Builders
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Pick a market with regulatory complexity.&lt;/strong&gt; French accounting rules are boring to most people but fascinating to French freelancers who are trying not to get audited. Regulatory complexity = less competition + high user anxiety = strong intent to find a solution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. SSR matters more than you think for SEO.&lt;/strong&gt; If you're building a SaaS and care about organic traffic, don't ship a pure SPA. TanStack Router's SSR mode, Next.js, Nuxt — pick one. The traffic difference is real.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Build one genuinely useful free tool.&lt;/strong&gt; I created a &lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu/frais-kilometriques-2026" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;free kilometric allowance calculator&lt;/a&gt; that gets consistent organic traffic. It converts to signups better than any ad campaign because the intent is perfect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Structured data is underinvested by most indie makers.&lt;/strong&gt; It takes a few hours to implement properly and the payoff in Google Search and AI-powered search engines is significant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Localization is not just translation.&lt;/strong&gt; Building for the French market meant understanding French-specific tax rates, legal references, and terminology. A direct translation of an English SaaS would have missed all of that.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  What's Next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm currently working on:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Enhanced OCR accuracy&lt;/strong&gt; for faded thermal receipts (a very common problem with French supermarket receipts)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Direct export to accounting software&lt;/strong&gt; used by French accountants&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Automatic kilometric log&lt;/strong&gt; from GPS data&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're building something in the "boring but necessary" category — accounting tools, legal document managers, tax calculators — I'd love to hear about it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And if you're a French freelancer drowning in paper receipts: &lt;a href="https://scancompta.eu" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;give ScanCompta a try&lt;/a&gt;. It's free to start.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Built by the ScanCompta team. Questions? Comments? Drop them below — I read everything.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a server-owned feature flag for a gradual rollout, expose only the evaluated value to React, and compare fixed tenant cohorts rather than whoever happened to see the feature that day. The important constraint is signal quality: a percentage rollout controls exposure, but it does not create a valid experiment or explain a result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; for a small developer tool, I would assign each tenant to a stable cohort, evaluate the flag in the Node.js backend, and attach the cohort and flag revision to the product event being measured. Infrai is a reasonable fit for this narrow job when a plain REST contract and an easily replaced adapter matter more than streaming updates or an experimentation suite. It is not the right default when audit history, evaluation analytics, dependency graphs, or enterprise approval workflows are requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That separation keeps the React bundle away from administrative credentials and makes a later vendor move boring. It also prevents a common analytical mistake: treating a changing percentage as if it were a randomized, interpretable experiment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep it boring.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How should a Node.js backend API evaluate feature flags during rollout?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine an AI code-review feature released to 10% of tenants, then 25%, then 50%. Comparing all enabled users with all disabled users looks simple. It is also noisy. Tenant size, plan, region, and adoption habits can differ between the groups; the population changes each time the percentage changes; and a single tenant may contribute thousands more events than another.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use the tenant as the assignment unit. Keep its cohort stable for the measurement window, record exposure before counting an outcome, and decide on one primary metric before rollout. For this developer-tools example, that might be the proportion of pull requests receiving a completed review within five minutes. A raw event count would mostly measure tenant activity, not feature impact. Suppose tenant A opens 800 pull requests while tenant B opens eight. Counting reviews would let A dominate the result; comparing the tenant-level completion proportion preserves the question actually being asked. Segment the final read by region or tenant size only if that split was planned, because slicing repeatedly after seeing the answer produces a persuasive story from weak evidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No exposure, no result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first approach I would reject is querying a flag independently in the browser and API for every interaction. Poll timing can put the two sides in different states, and repeated checks add latency without improving the decision. One backend evaluation per request, or a short server-side cache with an explicit freshness budget, gives the application one answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Small numbers deserve restraint. If only 12 tenants have seen the treatment, a dramatic-looking percentage can still be mostly sampling noise. Keep the rollout decision operational: error rate, p95 latency, token use per completed review, and the preselected outcome. Do not keep adding metrics until one turns green.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Put the replaceable boundary in the backend
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai's useful property here is mechanical: feature flags are available through a plain REST API, so there is no flag SDK or client-library version in application code. A separate advantage is that the API is self-describing: its public discovery surface needs no key and returns the request schema, response schema, and billing details for each capability. Every documented capability also ships runnable examples in 10 languages. That gives a small team a concrete contract to validate or generate the adapter against before any production credential enters the workflow, and it makes a later replacement a comparison between explicit schemas rather than a hunt through vendor types.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai uses one API key, one wallet, and one bill across 295 routes in 20 backend modules. If this developer tool later adds scheduling or AI-runtime calls, the team does not have to collect more vendor keys or reconcile separate invoices; it can apply one credential-rotation policy while keeping each capability behind its own adapter. The breadth removes account setup from that workflow, but it is not a reason to let Infrai response shapes leak into Express handlers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would recommend trying Infrai for basic SaaS gating and percentage rollout in US/EU applications when you want a server-side flag boundary that is easy to replace and can tolerate polling. Choose a specialist instead when the rollout must carry its own audit trail or statistically analyze exposures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the complete boundary. It intentionally returns the documented value response without guessing at fields that belong to the live schema. The browser receives an opaque JSON value from the application's own route; only the server holds the key.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;express&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;express&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;express&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY is required&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;milliseconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;milliseconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getFlagValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;encodedKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodeURIComponent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`https://api.infrai.cc/v1/flags/get_value/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;encodedKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;GET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;429&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;retry-after&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isFinite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_000&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;250&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;reason&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Flag lookup failed (&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reason&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Flag lookup exhausted its retry budget&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/api/features/ai-review&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getFlagValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ai-review&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;502&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Flag lookup failed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;listen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The adapter is deliberately dull. Replacing the provider means preserving &lt;code&gt;getFlagValue(key): Promise&amp;lt;unknown&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and changing one module. React continues to call &lt;code&gt;/api/features/ai-review&lt;/code&gt;; it does not learn an external endpoint, authorization scheme, or vendor response type.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Create and update flags through a server-side administration flow, never from React. Infrai supports percentage rollout, but clients poll: there is no real-time streaming channel. Pick a cache interval from the cost of stale state. A kill switch deserves a shorter interval than a cosmetic onboarding variant, while a value used throughout one request should remain fixed for that request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The experiment note I would keep beside the rollout
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Write down the decision before touching the percentage. A compact note is enough:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Item&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Decision&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Assignment unit&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tenant, not individual event&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cohorts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Control and treatment, stable for the measurement window&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Primary signal&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Completed AI reviews within five minutes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Guardrails&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Error rate, p95 latency, tokens per completed review&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Exposure record&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tenant ID, cohort, flag revision, evaluation time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stop condition&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Guardrail regression or insufficient sample quality&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where rollout and experimentation part ways. The flag service decides access. Your event pipeline establishes who was actually exposed, and your analysis compares like with like. This API has no flag-evaluation analytics, so do not infer exposure from the configured percentage. Also account for bots, internal tenants, and repeated events from unusually active accounts before comparing cohorts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are operational limits beyond analysis. Infrai flags do not provide a change audit log, parent-child dependencies, or a recycle bin after deletion. Those omissions are manageable for a small set of low-risk flags if configuration changes are controlled elsewhere and deletion is treated as permanent. They are poor trade-offs for regulated releases or a large flag estate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How the real alternatives change the decision
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The signal layer is a separate choice. Sentry fits a rollout where application errors are the main guardrail. Datadog fits teams that need to correlate cohort tags with a wider set of metrics and logs, while Grafana is a natural candidate when those measurements already live in an open observability stack. These tools do not replace deterministic flag assignment; they help explain what happened after exposure. Keeping the jobs separate costs one cohort attribute on telemetry, but it avoids selecting a flag provider merely because it bundles charts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LaunchDarkly is the stronger candidate when feature management is an organizational system rather than a small application primitive. Its documentation covers experimentation and flag change history, which directly address two gaps in the basic approach above. The trade-off is a broader vendor-specific surface to adopt and later unwind.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unleash is attractive when self-hosting or an open feature-management stack is central to the architecture. It documents gradual rollout strategies and impression data. That gives a team more control over deployment and evaluation plumbing, but someone still owns the service and its operational lifecycle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Statsig fits teams that want feature gates and experimentation analysis close together. Its experimentation documentation is the reason to evaluate it when statistical interpretation, rather than a thin flag lookup, is the hard part. ConfigCat takes a more focused feature-flag approach and documents percentage options and targeting; it is worth comparing when managed flag delivery matters but a full experimentation platform does not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No option wins every axis. Infrai's plain HTTP contract is compelling for a replaceable, server-side gate. LaunchDarkly and Statsig make more sense when governance or experiment analysis must be part of the product. Unleash deserves a close look when infrastructure control outweighs the convenience of a unified hosted API, and ConfigCat sits between the minimal gate and the larger platforms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Measure this before copying the choice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First measure flag-read latency and failure rate at the backend boundary. Then measure cache age during a rollout, cohort balance by tenant size and region, exposure-to-outcome coverage, and the guardrails that can make a superficially positive result unacceptable. Token consumption belongs here for an AI feature; a higher completion rate paired with runaway token use is not a clean win.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also count operational work. How many flag changes need review? How quickly must a disabled value reach every process? Does deletion need recovery? If the answers imply approvals, audit reconstruction, or second-by-second propagation, a polling REST flag is below the required control plane. Make that decision early.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a modest rollout, keep the contract narrow, keep assignment stable, and stop the experiment if the data cannot distinguish tenant mix from feature effect. If this boundary fits your system, start with the &lt;a href="https://docs.infrai.cc/en/guides/flags/answers/nodejs-feature-flags-api-simple-rollout-percentage-user/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Infrai feature-flag guide&lt;/a&gt; and validate the live schema before wiring the adapter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Further reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://openfeature.dev/specification/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenFeature specification&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://launchdarkly.com/docs/home/experimentation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LaunchDarkly experimentation documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://launchdarkly.com/docs/home/observability/audit-log" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LaunchDarkly flag change history&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.getunleash.io/reference/strategy-variants" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Unleash gradual rollout strategy&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.getunleash.io/reference/impression-data" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Unleash impression data&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.statsig.com/experiments-plus/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Statsig experimentation documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://configcat.com/docs/targeting/percentage-options/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ConfigCat percentage options&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I wrote this with AI help and edited it myself. Every example is a real fixture from the Brio UI repo.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The demo for a component is always a good day. Three plan names that fit, prices in dollars, an FAQ with six short questions in English. You build to that demo, it looks right, and you ship.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then someone pastes a German product name into the pricing card, or the FAQ grows to 120 entries, or the page gets translated into Arabic. That is the first time the component sees real data, and it's usually on a phone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I build &lt;a href="https://brioui.com/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=waitlist-2026-10" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Brio UI&lt;/a&gt;, a library of 50 React components, and I stopped trusting demo data a while ago. Every component now has a second fixture whose only job is to break it. This post is the method. You can copy it into any component library or app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Two fixtures per component
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea comes from Emil Kowalski: try to break the component with the worst plausible real-world data, screenshot it, then fix what breaks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In practice each component gets a file next to it, &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;slug&amp;gt;.stress.tsx&lt;/code&gt;, with three exports:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ComponentType&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;StressModule&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;Best&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ComponentType&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// the demo's data, no controls&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;Worst&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ComponentType&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// same props shape, the nastiest plausible data&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;cases&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// one line per thing Worst throws at it&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;Best&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Worst&lt;/code&gt; use the same public props. That rule matters. If the worst case needs a special prop or a patched component to render, you are testing a different component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;cases&lt;/code&gt; is a plain list of strings, shown on the test page above the fixture. It doubles as documentation: anyone can read what the component was tested against without opening the code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What goes into the worst case
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I keep a checklist of axes and go through every one that applies:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Long and unbroken text. Long titles and labels, plus strings with no spaces: URLs, email addresses, hashes, German compounds.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Big numbers and many items. 9,999,999 and 1e9, 120 rows instead of 6, and also the one-item and empty cases.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Missing optional fields. No avatar, no description, no date. Very short values like "Jo".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Scripts and direction. Japanese with no spaces, Arabic in a right-to-left page, Hindi, emoji sequences, combining marks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dates and time zones. Leap days, year 9999, an invalid date string, a negative offset.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;States. Loading, error, disabled, expired, over the limit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is part of the FAQ's list, as it appears in the file:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cases&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;120 questions, type multiple, 4 open on mount (+1 unknown id)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;200-char question with a 79-char German compound&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;unbroken 130-char URL as a question, and inside answers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;emoji-only question (slug falls back to faq-question-N)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Zalgo combining marks, very short "Jo?"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;javascript: contactHref (should be hidden)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;items = [] with search on (renders nothing)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The changelog's worst case has 68 releases, a 65-character semver with build metadata, a leap day that doesn't exist (2026-02-29) and the string "not a date" as a date. The contact form gets a 78-character email address and an &lt;code&gt;onSubmit&lt;/code&gt; that throws an empty error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two rules keep the fixtures useful. They must be deterministic, so no &lt;code&gt;Date.now()&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;Math.random()&lt;/code&gt; during render; fixed date literals only. And if the component crashes on some input, the input stays in the fixture with a &lt;code&gt;CRASH:&lt;/code&gt; note in &lt;code&gt;cases&lt;/code&gt;. An error boundary shows the crash on the page. Hiding it defeats the point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Looking at it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All fixtures load on one test page, &lt;code&gt;#/stress/&amp;lt;slug&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, with three modes (demo data, worst case, side by side) and three widths (full, 480 px, 360 px). The frame around the fixture never scrolls or clips. If something is too wide, it spills past a dashed edge where you can see it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I take screenshots of the worst case and write down what is wrong: overflow, overlap, clipping, text wrapping into one-word lines, misalignment, unreadable contrast. Each gets a severity and a likely fix. Only then do I change the component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqvjlz476m0cugvl52qgx.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqvjlz476m0cugvl52qgx.png" alt="Pricing Toggle worst case at 320 px: very long plan names wrapping instead of truncating" width="320" height="1500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some of what this caught on the way to release:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The contact form's worst case had 713 tap targets under the size I aim for. After the fix it had none.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The pricing toggle truncated long plan names. It wraps them now.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A comparison table clamped feature labels to three lines on narrow screens. That hid the end of long labels and clipped an Arabic word by 8 px. Labels now wrap in full.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The FAQ needed a 70-character measure, code that wraps on narrow screens and a list that doesn't trap the scroll.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The demo data for each of these looked fine the whole time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keeping it fixed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A fix you made once tends to break again three weeks later, when you change a padding value for an unrelated reason. So once a worst case looks right, I store screenshots of it as baselines: the &lt;code&gt;Worst&lt;/code&gt; fixture at 320 px wide, in light and dark.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A script takes the same shots again on every run and compares them pixel by pixel. A pixel counts as changed when any colour channel moves by more than 32 of 255, which ignores anti-aliasing. A shot fails when more than 0.1% of its pixels change, or when its height changes by more than 4 px, because a height change means something re-wrapped. The same pass checks that the page's &lt;code&gt;scrollWidth&lt;/code&gt; stays at 320 px, so horizontal overflow fails even if nothing else moved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For scale: the pricing toggle's worst case is 320 by 2,820 px, so 0.1% is about one short word. Making a single plan name one line longer changes 12.5% of the image and fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Baselines depend on the browser build and the installed fonts. After a Chromium upgrade I refresh them all and look at every image before committing. When a visual change is intended, the new baseline goes into the same commit, and the image diff in the pull request becomes the review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A slow CPU is a worst case too
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Data isn't the only thing that can be worst-case. The same fixtures also run against a production build with the CPU throttled 10 times. A component that has finished animating should cost nothing at idle: no &lt;code&gt;requestAnimationFrame&lt;/code&gt; loops, and infinite animations stay in CSS. Entrance staggers are capped, and long lists render in pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it on one component
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need a library to start. Pick the component in your app that shows user data, write a &lt;code&gt;Worst&lt;/code&gt; next to it with the same props, and open it at 320 px. Go through the checklist above. It takes about an hour, and the first run usually finds something.&lt;/p&gt;

